al government had  to do the things that was  constitutionally charged to do so that  the people could be free and prosper if  the federal government sought to do too  much those essential things that was  constitutionally required to do or in  danger of being left undone if you are  interested in reading the primary source  documents that define the debate that  we're going to be discussing today that  debate between the founding and the  progressive understanding of the  Constitution hillsdale college press has  just released a collection of 113  essential primary source documents it  captures that choice that is before us  today more information about the US  Constitution a reader is available  online at Hillsdale US Constitution calm  as you consider today's presentations by  hillsdale college president larry yarn  and hillsdale politics professors kevin  Porteous and rj post read oh please  submit your questions to them via email  at the email address town hall at  Hillsdale edu please include also your  name city and state our first speaker  today Senator Mike Lee has a  long-standing and strong attachment to  the principles of the American  Constitution a graduate of Brigham Young  University and also the BYU law school  mr. Lee served as a clerk for United  States Supreme Court Samuel Alito having  gone on to practice law and  distinguished private and public  capacities he saw our dearth of real  constitutional debate in his home state  of Utah and to redress this problem he  started article 1 societies across the  state gathering of citizens who were  interested in keeping government to its  constitutionally limited function in  2010 he was elected he was elected  united states senator from Utah becoming  that state's 16th senator a national  advocate for constitutionally limited  government he supports efforts to regain  fiscal responsibility individual liberty  and economic  disparity centered really is a member of  the Judiciary Committee the Foreign  R