nd article three vests the  judicial power of the United States in  one supreme court and in such inferior  courts as the Congress may from time to  time ordain and establish as the Supreme  Court has recently noted and repeatedly  noted the principle of separation of  powers was not just an abstract  generalization from the framers it was  woven into the document that they  drafted in Philadelphia and the  of 1787 they considered separation of  government powers essential to the  preservation of liberty in the Supreme  Court's words it was the central  judgment of the framers that within our  political scheme the separation of  government powers into three coordinate  branches is essential to the  preservation of Liberty James Madison  set up the principle of separation of  powers that no political truth is  certainly a greater intrinsic value or a  stamped with the authority of more  enlightened patrons of Liberty the  Constitution's separation of government  powers protects our Liberty in a number  of ways by dividing government power  into three branches each of which has  its own ambition for power the  Constitution ensures that different  branches may act as checks and balances  on each other this ensures that no one  branch will be able to exercise too much  government power separation of  government powers also protects against  any one branch exercising two  fundamental powers at the same time thus  no single branch can make a law and  execute or enforce that law and no  single branch can make a law and judge  whether that law has been violated the  framers foresaw the danger of such a  fusion of powers quoting the French  political thinker montesquieu who laid  the intellectual framework for  separation of powers James Madison wrote  in Federalist 47 when the legislative  and executive powers are united in the  same person or body there can be no  Liberty because apprehensions may arise  less the same body should enact  tyrannical laws to execute them in a