islators the legislative can have no  power to transfer their authority of  making laws and place it in other hands  in other words we the people elect  legislators to enact our nation's laws  that is a power that we give to our duly  elected legislators and a power the  Constitution does not allow them to give  to anyone else in the Supreme Court's  words that Congress cannot delegate  legislative power to the president is a  principle universally recognized as  vital to the integrity and maintenance  of the system of government  ordained by the Constitution this  constitutional principle protects our  liberty in a number of respects a few of  which I want to discuss briefly with you  right now first it limits the scope and  the volume of legislation one government  enacts laws that regulate what  individuals can and cannot do it  obviously restricts our basic Liberty  for that very reason the Constitution  created process that makes it quite  difficult to enact legislation Justice  Scalia explained that all those some see  all the disagreement and all the  argument that goes into the legislative  process and they tend to label that  dysfunctional the truth is that the  framers intentionally set up the  Constitution so that power would  contradict power and would create  legislative gridlock the framers did  this because as Alexander Hamilton noted  in Federalist number 62 the disease to  which our governments are most liable is  not a lack of legislation but rather the  excess of lawmaking the process for  enacting legislation in this country is  clearly spelled out in the constitution  article 1 section seven Clause 2 of the  Constitution provides that every bill  which shall have passed the House of  Representatives and the Senate shall  before it becomes law be presented to  the President of the United States as  the supreme court noted in I nsv chadda  the Constitution's prescription for  legislative action in article 1 sections  one and seven represents the framers  decision that the legislative power of  the federal government cannot or that it  must rather be exercised in accord with  a single finally rock and exhaustively  considered procedure but the government  does not operate according to this  single procedural the single procedure  involving bicameral passage and  presentment when it's an executive  branch an administrative agency like for  example the EPA that prom  gates law through regulation instead the  administrative agency must comply only  with certain procedures specified in a  statute known as the Administrative  Procedure Act that act specifies that an  agency must provide notice of its  intention to promulgate a law and must  allow for the public to comment on that  proposed law but the Administrative  Procedure Act imposes no real constraint  on what the agency can ultimately do in  fact a court reviewing an agency law can  strike it down only if the agency action  was arbitrary and capricious now it's  not very comforting to think that there  are tens of thousands of coercive laws  on the books that are considered valid  only because they were not so terrible  as to be judged arbitrary and capricious  and of course the result of all this is  that we have many many more national  laws on the books than the framers ever  intended us to have in some of the  Constitution's intentionally onerous  procedure for enacting legislative  legislation and it's bicameralism  principles cannot protect us from  excessive legislation if the government  simply ignores that required procedure  mandated by the Constitution another way  the Constitution's requirement that only  Congress exercise legislative power  protects our Liberty is by ensuring that  the democratic process works properly  such that the government is accountable  for the laws and an axe as Chief Justice  William Rehnquist explained by requiring  the elected legislature to make our laws  we ensure that important choices of  social policy are made by Congress the  branch of government that are that is  most responsive to the popular will the  Supreme Court echoed that sentiment in  insp chadha where it stated by providing  that no law could take effect without  the concurrence of the prescribed  majority of the members of both the  houses of Congress the framers  reemphasize their belief that  legislation should not be enacted unless  it has been carefully and fully  considered by the nation's elected  officials this is an important point if  Congress enacts a really bad law  citizens will notice and they may choose  to retire members of Congress who voted  for that  law in that respect that people can hold  their legislators accountable for the  misuse of government power and on the  other hand when executive agencies  overstep their bounds and do not simply  execute and enforce the laws but also  make laws it's not clear what citizens  can do about it the bureaucrats who make  up administrative agencies are not  elected so that people can't vote them  out of office parenthetically this same  separation of powers problem occurs when  judges go beyond their bounds and they  legislate from the bench federal judges  are not elected and thus should not be  making federal policy and law despite  these and other important considerations  behind the framers decision to vest the  legislative power in the Congress alone  when we look at all the laws and  regulations that are passed in this  country each year most of them most of  the nation's legislative powers are  exercised not by our elected legislature  the Congress but rather by  administrative agencies that are not  part that are part of the executive  branch the Federal Register which  publishes the laws and regulations  promulgated by administrative agencies  each year grew by over 80,000 pages just  this last year by contrast the US Code  which publishes laws enacted by Congress  grew by less than 3,000 pages the  majority of our country's laws are not  made 