 bulk  of what administrative agencies do today  is legislative activity even some  liberal members of the Supreme Court  have stated that we should no longer  pretend that agencies do not legislate  and should instead simply admit that  agency rulemaking authority is  legislative power these justices  correctly noted that if the rules that  agencies so often promulgate had been  prescribed by Congress everyone would  agree that those rules would be the  product and of an exercise of  legislative power it's therefore clear  that the current administrative state  with its aggressive exercise of  legislative power violates the  Constitution's separation of powers  principle and as can be expected one  Constitution's safeguard safeguards are  ignored this practice has some dire  consequences because bureaucrats cannot  be effectively held accountable and they  seem to measure their success by the  number of new laws and regulations they  can enact businesses and citizens alike  are left swimming in a sea of seemingly  unending laws restrictions and rules  this over-regulation subsumes the free  market imposing paperwork roadblocks and  costs on businesses present preventing  the growth of our economy that it needs  to provide jobs for those seeking work  in all this very little is known or  understood about what these massive  administrative agencies are doing at any  given time and why they're doing so much  in saying this I must note that I am far  from the only person who fears the  consequences of our big bad and  unaccountable administrative state to  take but one example a highly cited  article in the Harvard Law Review from  2001 said the following bureaucracy is  the ultimate black box of government the  place where exercises of coercive power  are most unfathomable and thus most  threatening to a great extent this will  always be so the bureaucratic form and  its proportions its reach in its  distances impervious to full public  understanding much much less control  that article was written by elena kagan  now a member of the supreme court i can  think safely that Justice Kagan and I do  not agree on all political issues but  apparently we do agree on the threat  posed by the administrative state to  constrain the overwhelming appetites of  the administrative state and the threat  to Liberty it constitutes we must  enforce the Constitution separation of  government powers and insist that  Congress alone exercise the government's  proper legislative power in doing this  we cannot rely on the courts alone the  Constitution nowhere suggests that the  judiciary is the only branch charged  with faithful interpretation and  implementation of the Constitution  indeed the framers would have been  astonished at such a notion to the  contrary Thomas Jefferson wrote that the  opinion which gives to the judges their  right to decide what laws are  constitutional and what or not not only  for themselves within their own sphere  of action but for the legislature and  the executive also and their spheres  would make the judiciary a despotic  branch so far from assigning  interpretation and protection of the  Constitution to the judicial branch the  Constitution mandates that the President  and each member of Congress take an oath  to be faithful to the Constitution it's  the duty of all three branches  faithfully to interpret and implement  the Constitution's limits on power with  that in mind we as citizens must hold  Congress and the president accountable  for their treatment of the Constitution  separation of powers Congress must not  pass any more laws that broadly delegate  legislative power to unelected agencies  and to the extent possible we must hold  the president accountable for the  excessive volume of regulations  promulgated by administrative agencies  within the executive branch Congress  must also take measures to regain the  legislative powers that past congresses  have unconstitutionally delegated to  executive agencies one such measure of  which I am an original co-sponsor in the  Senate is the reins Act arraigns is  short for regulations from the executive  in need of scrutiny the Act would  mandate that any rule promulgated by an  executive agency that would have an  annual economic impact of one hundred  million dollars or more must receive a  vote by Congress if a majority of each  house of Congress does not vote in favor  of that significant regulation or the  president doesn't sign it into law it  will not go into effect the reins act  has already passed in the House of  Representatives and I'm hopeful it'll  also pass in the Senate I believe this  enactment of this legislation would be a  very important first step toward  regaining some of that Liberty we've  lost as a result of the expansion of the  administrative state it hardly seems  controversial to require the Congress  and the president to account for the  significant exercises of legislative  power represented by major  administrative actions in fact the  current situation is such that if we  were to proceed without the reins act  we'd be in bad bad shape it's absolutely  essential that we constrain this  coercive power  that the administrative state has on the  US economy so many dollars of us  industry each year have been expended  complying with this work and and we have  to rein in this work that's been  accomplished by unelected bureaucrats  who in so many cases are part of  so-called independent agencies that not  even the President of the United States  himself controls this is a dangerous  situation with so many out of work in an  economy stagnated by burdensome  regulation we have to do what we can to  reduce the size of the administrative  state stimulate our economy and regain  the Liberty that has made America the  envy of the world we have to elect  representatives who will consciously  interpret the Constitution and make sure  that we stick to the original meaning we  have to elect a president that will do  the same and who will appoint judges who  will also respect those limits we can  restore this vision above the founding  fathers to restore limited power and  safeguard the liberty of our founding  fathers that was purchased for us each  of us at so great a price but in order  to do that we have to vote we have to  speak our minds and most importantly we  have to reclaim that birthright which is  ours which is created by the  Constitution that birthright cannot take  full effect unless or until we demand  that laws are passed in the manner  prescribed by article 1 sections one and  seven of the US Constitution if we'll do  these things we will again regain our  liberty and our greatness as a nation  thank you

