islators the legislative can have no  power to transfer their authority of  making laws and place it in other hands  in other words we the people elect  legislators to enact our nation's laws  that is a power that we give to our duly  elected legislators and a power the  Constitution does not allow them to give  to anyone else in the Supreme Court's  words that Congress cannot delegate  legislative power to the president is a  principle universally recognized as  vital to the integrity and maintenance  of the system of government  ordained by the Constitution this  constitutional principle protects our  liberty in a number of respects a few of  which I want to discuss briefly with you  right now first it limits the scope and  the volume of legislation one government  enacts laws that regulate what  individuals can and cannot do it  obviously restricts our basic Liberty  for that very reason the Constitution  created process that makes it quite  difficult to enact legislation Justice  Scalia explained that all those some see  all the disagreement and all the  argument that goes into the legislative  process and they tend to label that  dysfunctional the truth is that the  framers intentionally set up the  Constitution so that power would  contradict power and would create  legislative gridlock the framers did  this because as Alexander Hamilton noted  in Federalist number 62 the disease to  which our governments are most liable is  not a lack of legislation but rather the  excess of lawmaking the process for  enacting legislation in this country is  clearly spelled out in the constitution  article 1 section seven Clause 2 of the  Constitution provides that every bill  which shall have passed the House of  Representatives and the Senate shall  before it becomes law be presented to  the President of the United States as  the supreme court noted in I nsv chadda  the Constitution's prescription for  legislative action in article 1 sections  one and seven represents the framers  decision that the legislative power of  the federal government cannot or that it  must rather be exercised in accord with  a single finally rock and exhaustively  considered procedure but the government  does not operate according to this  single procedural the single procedure  involving bicameral passage and  presentment when it's an executive  branch an administrative agency like for  example the EPA that prom  gates law through regulation instead the  administrative agency must comply only  with certain procedures specified in a  statute known as the Administrative  Procedure Act that act specifies that an  agency must provide notice of its  intention to promulgate a law and must  allow for the public to comment on that  proposed law but the Administrative  Procedure Act imposes no real constraint  on what the agency can ultimately do in  fact a court reviewing an agency law can  strike it down only if the agency action  was arbitrary and capricious now it's  not very comforting to think that there  are tens of thousands of coercive laws  on the books that are considered valid  only because they were not so terrible  as to be judged arbitrary and capricious  and of course the result of all this is  that we have many many more national  laws on the books than the framers ever  intended us to have in some of the  Constitution's intentionally onerous  procedure for enacting legislative  legislation and it's bicameralism  principles cannot protect us from  excessive legislation if the government  simply ignores that required procedure  mandated by the Constitution another way  the Constitution's requirement that only  Congress exercise legislative power  protects our Liberty is by ensuring that  the democratic process works properly  such that the government is accountable  for the laws and an axe as Chief Justice  William Rehnquist explained by requiring  the elected legislature to make our laws  we ensure that important choices of  social policy are made by Congress the  branch of government that are that is  most responsive to the popular will the  Supreme Court echoed that sentiment in  insp chadha where it stated by providing  that no law could take effect without  the concurrence of the prescribed  majority of the members of both the  houses of Congress the framers  reemphasize their belief that  legislation should not be enacted unless  it has been carefully and fully  considered by the nation's elected  officials this is an important point if  Congress enacts a really bad law  citizens will notice and they may choose  to retire members of Congress who voted  for that  law in that respect that people can hold  their legislators accountable for the  misuse of government power and on the  other hand when executive agencies  overstep their bounds and do not simply  execute and enforce the laws but also  make laws it's not clear what citizens  can do about it the bureaucrats who make  up administrative agencies are not  elected so that people can't vote them  out of office parenthetically this same  separation of powers problem occurs when  judges go beyond their bounds and they  legislate from the bench federal judges  are not elected and thus should not be  making federal policy and law despite  these and other important considerations  behind the framers decision to vest the  legislative power in the Congress alone  when we look at all the laws and  regulations that are passed in this  country each year most of them most of  the nation's legislative powers are  exercised not by our elected legislature  the Congress but rather by  administrative agencies that are not  part that are part of the executive  branch the Federal Register which  publishes the laws and regulations  promulgated by administrative agencies  each year grew by over 80,000 pages just  this last year by contrast the US Code  which publishes laws enacted by Congress  grew by less than 3,000 pages the  majority of our country's laws are not  made by our elected legislature but  rather by unelected bureaucrats and this  is a problem perhaps some would quibble  with the definition of legislative  activity and argue that administrative  agencies are not necessarily legislating  but rather simply administering broad  congressional enactments in fact over  time and after countless attempts by  Congress to delegate much of its  legislative power to these agencies even  the Supreme Court has acquiesced to this  view and allowed broad delegations of  power to executive branch agencies as  long as Congress provides that agency  and intelligible principle to guide its  actions of course no one really disputes  that it's constitutionally proper for  administrative agencies to execute and  enforce  our country's laws indeed those agencies  may even need to make regulations  necessary to enforce the law whether  that be specifying the hours the lights  will come on in various department  buildings or the manner and procedure  for enforcing the laws unfortunately our  country's administrative agencies have  gone well beyond these limits and there  can be little dispute that much of what  they do is legislative activity when  which rightfully belongs only to the  legislative branch Congress although  there is no single definition of what  constitutes legislative activity most  would agree that to legislate is to make  generally applicable rules that have the  force of law for example in insp chaddha  the supreme court stated that a  government action was legislative  because it had the purpose and effective  altering the legal rights duties and  relations of persons outside the  legislative branch under that definition  the actions of administrative agencies  easily qualify as legislative every year  administrative agencies promulgate  hundreds if not thousands of laws that  have the effect of altering the legal  rights duties and relations of all  citizens but regardless of the precise  definition used it's plain that the bulk  of what administrative agencies do today  is legislative activity even some  liberal members of the Supreme Court  have stated that we should no longer  pretend that agencies do not legislate  and should instead simply admit that  agency rulemaking authority is  legislative power these justices  correctly noted that if the rules that  agencies so often promulgate had been  prescribed by Congress everyone would  agree that those rules would be the  product and of an exercise of  legislative power it's therefore clear  that the current administrative state  with its aggressive exercise of  legislative power violates the  Constitution's separation of powers  principle and as can be expected one  Constitution's safeguard safeguards are  ignored this practice has some dire  consequences because bureaucrats cannot  be effectively held accountable and they  seem to measure their success by the  number of new laws and regulations they  can enact businesses and citizens alike  are left swimming in a sea of seemingly  unending laws restrictions and rules  this over-regulation subsumes the free  market imposing paperwork roadblocks and  costs on businesses present preventing  the growth of our economy that it needs  to provide jobs for those seeking work  in all this very little is known or  understood about what these massive  administrative agencies are doing at any  given time and why they're doing so much  in saying this I must note that I am far  from the only person who fears the  consequences of our big bad and  unaccountable administrative state to  take but one example a highly cited  article in the Harvard Law Review from  2001 said the following bureaucracy is  the ultimate black box of government the  place where exercises of coercive power  are most unfathomable and thus most  threatening to a great extent this will  always be so the bureaucratic form and  its proportions its reach in its  distances impervious to full public  understanding much much less control  that article was written by elena kagan  now a member of the supreme court i can  think safely that Justice Kagan and I do  not agree on all political issues but  apparently we do agree on the threat  posed by the administrative state to  constrain the overwhelming appetites of  the administrative state and the threat  to Liberty it constitutes we must  enforce the Constitution separation of  government powers and insist that  Congress alone exercise the government's  proper legislative power in doing this  we cannot rely on the courts alone the  Constitution nowhere suggests that the  judiciary is the only branch charged  with faithful interpretation and  implementation of the Constitution  indeed the framers would have been  astonished at such a notion to the  contrary Thomas Jefferson wrote that the  opinion which gives to the judges their  right to decide what laws are  constitutional and what or not not only  for themselves within their own sphere  of action but for the legislature and  the executive also and their spheres  would make the judiciary a despotic  branch so far from assigning  interpretation and protection of the  Constitution to the judicial branch the  Constitution mandates that the President  and each member of Congress take an oath  to be faithful to the Constitution it's  the duty of all three branches  faithfully to interpret and implement  the Constitution's limits on power with  that in mind we as citizens must hold  Congress and the president accountable  for their treatment of the Constitution  separation of powers Congress must not  pass any more laws that broadly delegate  legislative power to unelected agencies  and to the extent possible we must hold  the president accountable for the  excessive volume of regulations  promulgated by administrative agencies  within the executive branch Congress  must also take measures to regain the  legislative powers that past congresses  have unconstitutionally delegated to  executive agencies one such measure of  which I am an original co-sponsor in the  Senate is the reins Act arraigns is  short for regulations from the executive  in need of scrutiny the Act would  mandate that any rule promulgated by an  executive agency that would have an  annual economic impact of one hundred  million dollars or more must receive a  vote by Congress if a majority of each  house of Congress does not vote in favor  of that significant regulation or the  president doesn't sign it into law it  will not go into effect the reins act  has already passed in the House of  Representatives and I'm hopeful it'll  also pass in the Senate I believe this  enactment of this legislation would be a  very important first step toward  regaining some of that Liberty we've  lost as a result of the expansion of the  administrative state it hardly seems  controversial to require the Congress  and the president to account for the  significant exercises of legislative  power represented by major  administrative actions in fact the  current situation is such that if we  were to proceed without the reins act  we'd be in bad bad shape it's absolutely  essential that we constrain this  coercive power  that the administrative state has on the  US economy so many dollars of us  industry each year have been expended  complying with this work and and we have  to rein in this work that's been  accomplished by unelected bureaucrats  who in so many cases are part of  so-called independent agencies that not  even the President of the United States  himself controls this is a dangerous  situation with so many out of work in an  economy stagnated by burdensome  regulation we have to do what we can to  reduce the size of the administrative  state stimulate our economy and regain  the Liberty that has made America the  envy of the world we have to elect  representatives who will consciously  interpret the Constitution and make sure  that we stick to the original meaning we  have to elect a president that will do  the same and who will appoint judges who  will also respect those limits we can  restore this vision above the founding  fathers to restore limited power and  safeguard the liberty of our founding  fathers that was purchased for us each  of us at so great a price but in order  to do that we have to vote we have to  speak our minds and most importantly we  have to reclaim that birthright which is  ours which is created by the  Constitution that birthright cannot take  full effect unless or until we demand  that laws are passed in the manner  prescribed by article 1 sections one and  seven of the US Constitution if we'll do  these things we will again regain our  liberty and our greatness as a nation  thank you
 

